# SYNOPSIS

```swift
import PixelKernels

enum PixelKernels {
  static func fill(_ target: UnsafeMutablePointer<UInt32>, _ count: Int,
                   _ color: UInt32)
  static func copyOpaque(_ target: UnsafeMutablePointer<UInt32>,
                         _ source: UnsafePointer<UInt32>, _ count: Int)
  static func over(premultiplied source: UnsafePointer<UInt32>,
                   _ target: UnsafeMutablePointer<UInt32>, _ count: Int)
  static func over(straight source: UnsafePointer<UInt32>,
                   _ target: UnsafeMutablePointer<UInt32>, _ count: Int)
  static func over(_ color: UInt32, coverage: UInt32,
                   _ target: UnsafeMutablePointer<UInt32>, _ count: Int)
  static func overText(_ ink: inout TextInk, coverage: UnsafePointer<UInt8>,
                       _ target: UnsafeMutablePointer<UInt32>, _ count: Int,
                       rows: Int = 1, coverageStride: Int = 0,
                       targetStride: Int = 0)

  struct TextInk {
    init()
    mutating func set(_ color: UInt32, alpha: UInt32, contrast: TextContrast)
    mutating func keepGlyphs()
  }

  enum Reference { /* the same six, a pixel at a time; overText takes
                      the ink's colour, alpha and contrast */ }
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

A kernel works on a span: `count` pixels in a row from `target`, and from `source` where there is one.
A caller hands a whole row, or the part of it a clip leaves, rather than a pixel at a time.
Pixels are 32-bit words, `0x00RRGGBB`, or `0xAARRGGBB` where the source has alpha.

`fill` sets each pixel to `color`.
`copyOpaque` copies each pixel with its top byte cleared.
`over(premultiplied:)` and `over(straight:)` draw each source pixel over the target's, in linear light, as light(2)'s functions of the same names do, and leave the target's top bytes clear.
`over(_:coverage:)` draws one colour over each pixel at one coverage, as light(2)'s `over` does.
`overText` draws `rows` rows of a glyph, `count` pixels each, its coverage a byte a pixel and its rows `coverageStride` bytes apart over target rows `targetStride` pixels apart, so a glyph goes in one call, in a `TextInk`: its colour through its `contrast` curve at its `alpha`, as light(2)'s `overText` does, set with `set` (a new ink is black at alpha 0); it leaves the pixels it doesn't cover as they are.
An ink keeps the pixels it gave at each coverage for the last four colours, alphas and contrasts over a ground that it met, so a renderer keeps one through a frame, and text that goes between a few colours on a few grounds blends each coverage once in each; a fifth gives up the one longest kept.
After `keepGlyphs`, an ink also keeps each glyph it draws over a ground all one pixel, found by its coverage's address and size, its colour, alpha and contrast, and the ground, and the next time it meets that glyph there it copies the pixels it left, with no blend; a glyph over a ground that varies is drawn as before.
So coverage at an address must not change while the ink is kept: UICore's renderer keeps glyphs through a frame, while its atlas is fixed.
It keeps 65,536 pixels of glyphs at most, found through 1,024 entries; past either, a glyph is drawn and not kept.

Each kernel gives exactly the bytes its `Reference` counterpart gives from the same inputs; the reference draws a pixel at a time through light(2).
The kernels take eight pixels a step: a fill or a copy whole, and a blend whole where all eight source pixels are opaque, or are clear, and through light(2) one pixel at a time otherwise.
A colour at one coverage is a fill at full coverage, and is blended once for a run of pixels alike under it; a glyph's row is taken eight coverages at a time, blending only those not clear.
The `pixels` suite checks each against the reference, over spans of every length to 40 at several offsets, the text kernel in one ink throughout over grounds that come round again, a row at a time and in blocks of rows, and an ink that keeps glyphs over grounds alike and not, met again in other inks.

Radharc's composer draws its fills and its windows' images through them (radharc(4)), and UICore's renderer its shapes' insides and its glyphs, through text(2)'s span methods on a `PixelTarget` (uicore(2)).

